## an3.35
an3.35:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: (none)
an3.35:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Āḷavī, on a mat of leaves by a cow-path in a rosewood grove. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Āḷavī on a heap of leaves spread out on a cow track in a siṁsapā grove.
an3.35:1.3 | S: Then as Hatthaka of Āḷavī was going for a walk he saw the Buddha sitting on that mat of leaves. | T: Then Hatthaka of Āḷavī, while walking and wandering for exercise, saw the Blessed One sitting there.
an3.35:1.4 | S: He went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said, | T: He then approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to the Blessed One:
an3.35:1.5 | S: “Sir, I trust the Buddha slept well?” | T: “Bhante, did the Blessed One sleep well?”
an3.35:1.6 | S: “Yes, prince, I slept well. | T: “Yes, prince, I slept well.
an3.35:1.7 | S: I am one of those who sleep at ease in the world.” | T: I am one of those in the world who sleep well.”
an3.35:2.1 | S: “The winter nights are cold, sir, and it’s the week of mid-winter, when the snow falls. Rough is the ground trampled under the cows’ hooves, and thin is the mat of leaves. The leaves are sparse on the trees, the ocher robes are cold, and cold blows the north wind. | T: “But, Bhante, the winter nights are cold. It is the eight-day interval, the time when snow falls. The ground trampled by the hooves of cattle is rough, the spread of leaves is thin, the leaves on the tree are sparse, the ochre robes leave one cold, and the gale wind blows cold.
an3.35:2.2 | S: And yet the Buddha says, | T: Yet the Blessed One says thus:
an3.35:2.3 | S: ‘Yes, prince, I slept well. | T: ‘Yes, prince, I slept well.
an3.35:2.4 | S: I am one of those who sleep at ease in the world.’” | T: I am one of those in the world who sleep well.’”
an3.35:3.1 | S: “Well then, prince, I’ll ask you about this in return, and you can answer as you like. | T: “Well then, prince, I will question you about this matter. You should answer as you see fit.
an3.35:3.2 | S: What do you think? Take the case of a householder or his son, who lives in a bungalow, plastered inside and out, draft-free, with door fastened and window shuttered. | T: What do you think, prince? A householder or a householder’s son might have a house with a peaked roof, plastered inside and out, draft-free, with bolts fastened and shutters closed.
an3.35:3.3 | S: His couch is spread with woolen covers—shag-piled, pure white, or embroidered with flowers—and spread with a fine deer hide. It has a canopy above and red pillows at both ends. | T: There he might have a couch spread with rugs, blankets, and covers, with an excellent covering of antelope hide, with a canopy above and red bolsters at both ends.
an3.35:3.4 | S: An oil lamp is burning there, | T: An oil lamp would be burning and his four wives would serve him in extremely agreeable ways.
an3.35:3.5 | S: while his four wives attend to him in all manner of agreeable ways. | T: (none)
an3.35:3.6 | S: What do you think, prince, would he sleep at ease, or not? | T: What do you think, would he sleep well or not, or what do you think about this?”
an3.35:3.7 | S: Or how do you see this?” | T: (none)
an3.35:3.8 | S: “He would sleep at ease, sir. | T: “He would sleep well, Bhante.
an3.35:3.9 | S: Of those who sleep at ease in the world, he would be one.” | T: He would be one of those in the world who sleep well.”
an3.35:4.1 | S: “What do you think, prince? | T: (1) “What do you think, prince?
an3.35:4.2 | S: Is it not possible that a fever born of greed—physical or mental—might arise in that householder or householder’s son, burning him so he sleeps badly?” | T: Might there arise in that householder or householder’s son bodily and mental fevers born of lust, which would torment him so that he would sleep badly?”
an3.35:4.3 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, Bhante.”
an3.35:5.1 | S: “The greed that burns that householder or householder’s son, making them sleep badly, has been cut off at the root by the Realized One, made like a palm stump, obliterated, and unable to arise in the future. | T: “There might arise in that householder or householder’s son bodily and mental fevers born of lust, which would torment him so that he would sleep badly; but the Tathāgata has abandoned such lust, cut it off at the root, made it like a palm stump, obliterated it so that it is no more subject to future arising.
an3.35:5.2 | S: That’s why I sleep at ease. | T: Therefore I have slept well.
an3.35:6.1 | S: What do you think, prince? | T: (2) “What do you think, prince?
an3.35:6.2 | S: Is it not possible that a fever born of hate … | T: Might there arise in that householder or householder’s son bodily and mental fevers born of hatred …
an3.35:6.3 | S: or a fever born of delusion—physical or mental—might arise in that householder or householder’s son, burning him so he sleeps badly?” | T: (3) … bodily and mental fevers born of delusion, which would torment him so that he would sleep badly?”
an3.35:6.4 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, Bhante.”
an3.35:7.1 | S: “The delusion that burns that householder or householder’s son, making them sleep badly, has been cut off at the root by the Realized One, made like a palm stump, obliterated, and unable to arise in the future. | T: “There might arise in that householder or householder’s son bodily and mental fevers born of delusion, which would torment him so that he would sleep badly; but the Tathāgata has abandoned such delusion, cut it off at the root, made it like a palm stump, obliterated it so that it is no more subject to future arising.
an3.35:7.2 | S: That’s why I sleep at ease. | T: Therefore I have slept well.”
an3.35:8.1 | S: A brahmin who is fully quenched | T: He always sleeps well, the brahmin who has attained nibbāna,
an3.35:8.2 | S: always sleeps at ease. | T: cooled off, without acquisitions,
an3.35:8.3 | S: Sensual pleasures slip off them, | T: not tainted by sensual pleasures.
an3.35:8.4 | S: they’re cooled, free of attachments. | T: (none)
an3.35:9.1 | S: Since they’ve cut off all clinging, | T: Having cut off all attachments,
an3.35:9.2 | S: and removed the stress from the heart, | T: having removed anguish in the heart,
an3.35:9.3 | S: the peaceful sleep at ease, | T: the peaceful one sleeps well,
an3.35:9.4 | S: having found peace of mind.” | T: having attained peace of mind.
an3.35:9.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

