## sn35.132
sn35.132:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Mahākaccāna was staying in the land of the Avantis in a wilderness hut near Makkarakaṭa. | T: On one occasion the Venerable Mahakaccana was dwelling among the people of Avanti in a forest hut at Makkarakaṭa.
sn35.132:1.2 | S: Then several students, pupils of the brahmin Lohicca, approached Mahākaccāna’s wilderness hut while collecting firewood. They walked and wandered all around the hut, making a colossal racket and all kinds of jeers: | T: Then a number of brahmin youths, students of the brahmin Lohicca, while collecting firewood, approached the Venerable Mahakaccana’s forest hut. Having approached, they stomped and trampled all around the hut, and in a boisterous and noisy manner they played various pranks, saying:
sn35.132:1.3 | S: “These shavelings, fake ascetics, primitives, black spawn from the feet of our kinsman, the Lord! They’re honored, respected, revered, venerated, and esteemed by so-called inheritors of Vedic culture.” | T: “These shaveling ascetics, menials, swarthy offspring of the Lord’s feet, are honoured, respected, esteemed, worshipped, and venerated by their servile devotees.”
sn35.132:1.4 | S: Then Mahākaccāna left his dwelling and said to those young students, | T: Then the Venerable Mahakaccana came out of his dwelling and said to those brahmin youths:
sn35.132:1.5 | S: “Young students, stop being so noisy. | T: “Don’t make any noise, boys.
sn35.132:1.6 | S: I will speak to you on the teaching.” | T: I will speak to you on the Dhamma.”
sn35.132:1.7 | S: When this was said, the young students fell silent. | T: When this was said, those youths became silent.
sn35.132:1.8 | S: Then Mahākaccāna recited these verses for them: | T: Then the Venerable Mahakaccana addressed those youths with verses:
sn35.132:2.1 | S: “For the brahmins of old, ethics were the highest; | T: “Those men of old who excelled in virtue,
sn35.132:2.2 | S: they remembered the ancient legends. | T: Those brahmins who recalled the ancient rules,
sn35.132:2.3 | S: Their sense doors were guarded, well protected, | T: Their sense doors guarded, well protected,
sn35.132:2.4 | S: and they had mastered anger. | T: Dwelt having vanquished wrath within.
sn35.132:3.1 | S: Those brahmins who remembered <j>the ancient legends | T: They took delight in Dhamma and meditation, Those brahmins who recalled the ancient rules.
sn35.132:3.2 | S: enjoyed virtue and absorption. | T: (none)
sn35.132:4.1 | S: But these have lost their way. <j>Saying, ‘We must pray’, | T: “But these have fallen, claiming ‘We recite.’
sn35.132:4.2 | S: they live out of balance, judging by clan. | T: Puffed up by clan, faring unrighteously,
sn35.132:4.3 | S: Mastered by anger, they take up many arms, | T: Overcome by anger, armed with diverse weapons,
sn35.132:4.4 | S: indifferent to those with craving and without. | T: They molest both frail and firm.
sn35.132:5.1 | S: All is vain for someone <j>who doesn’t guard the sense doors, | T: “For one with sense doors unguarded All the vows he undertakes are vain
sn35.132:5.2 | S: like the wealth a person finds in a dream. | T: Just like the wealth a man gains in a dream:
sn35.132:5.3 | S: Fasting, sleeping on bare ground, | T: Fasting and sleeping on the ground,
sn35.132:5.4 | S: bathing at daybreak, the three Vedas, | T: Bathing at dawn, study of the three Vedas,
sn35.132:6.1 | S: rough hides, dreadlocks, and mud, | T: Rough hides, matted locks, and dirt;
sn35.132:6.2 | S: hymns, precepts and observances, <j>and fervent austerities, | T: Hymns, rules and vows, austerities,
sn35.132:6.3 | S: fawning, bent staffs, | T: Hypocrisy, bent staffs,
sn35.132:6.4 | S: and sipping water. | T: ablutions:
sn35.132:6.5 | S: These emblems of the brahmins | T: These emblems of the brahmins
sn35.132:6.6 | S: are only used to generate profits. | T: Are used to increase their worldly gains.
sn35.132:7.1 | S: A mind that’s serene, | T: “A mind that is well concentrated,
sn35.132:7.2 | S: clear and unclouded, | T: Clear and free from blemish,
sn35.132:7.3 | S: kind to all creatures: | T: Tender towards all sentient beings—
sn35.132:7.4 | S: that’s the path to attainment of divinity!” | T: That is the path for attaining Brahma.”
sn35.132:8.1 | S: Then those young students, offended and upset, went to the brahmin Lohicca and said to him, | T: Then those brahmin youths, angry and displeased, approached the brahmin Lohicca and told him:
sn35.132:8.2 | S: “Please, master, you should know this. The ascetic Mahākaccāna condemns and rejects outright the hymns of the brahmins!” | T: “See now, sir, you should know that the ascetic Mahakaccana categorically denigrates and scorns the hymns of the brahmins.”
sn35.132:8.3 | S: When they said this, Lohicca was offended and upset. | T: When this was said, the brahmin Lohicca was angry and displeased.
sn35.132:8.4 | S: Then he thought, | T: But then it occurred to him:
sn35.132:8.5 | S: “But it wouldn’t be appropriate for me to abuse or insult the ascetic Mahākaccāna solely because of what I’ve heard from these young students. | T: “It is not proper for me to abuse and revile the ascetic Mahakaccana solely on the basis of what I have heard from these youths.
sn35.132:8.6 | S: Why don’t I go and ask him about it?” | T: Let me approach him and inquire.”
sn35.132:9.1 | S: Then the brahmin Lohicca together with those young students went to Venerable Mahākaccāna and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then the brahmin Lohicca, together with those brahmin youths, approached the Venerable Mahakaccana. He exchanged greetings with the Venerable Mahakaccana and,
sn35.132:9.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to him, | T: when they had concluded their greetings and cordial talk, he sat down to one side and said to him:
sn35.132:9.3 | S: “Master Kaccāna, did several young pupils of mine come by here collecting firewood?” | T: “Master Kaccana, did a number of brahmin youths, my students, come this way while collecting firewood?”
sn35.132:9.4 | S: “They did, brahmin.” | T: “They did, brahmin.”
sn35.132:9.5 | S: “But did you have some discussion with them?” | T: “Did Master Kaccana have any conversation with them?”
sn35.132:9.6 | S: “I did.” | T: “I did have a conversation with them, brahmin.”
sn35.132:9.7 | S: “But what kind of discussion did you have with them?” | T: “What kind of conversation did you have with them, Master Kaccana?”
sn35.132:9.8 | S: “This is the discussion I had with these young students.” And he repeated the verses in full. | T: “The conversation I had with those youths was like this:
sn35.132:10.1 | P: ‘Sīluttamā pubbatarā ahesuṁ, | T: (none)
sn35.132:10.2 | P: Te brāhmaṇā ye purāṇaṁ saranti; | T: “‘Those men of old who excelled in virtue, Those brahmins who recalled the ancient rules,
sn35.132:10.3 | P: …pe… | T: …
sn35.132:10.4 | P: Akhilaṁ sabbabhūtesu, | T: Tender towards all sentient beings—
sn35.132:10.5 | P: So maggo brahmapattiyā’ti. | T: That is the path for attaining Brahma.’
sn35.132:11.1 | P: Evaṁ kho me, brāhmaṇa, tehi māṇavakehi saddhiṁ ahosi kathāsallāpo”ti. | T: Such was the conversation that I had with those youths.”
sn35.132:12.1 | S: “Worthy Kaccāna spoke of someone who doesn’t guard the sense doors. | T: “Master Kaccana said ‘with sense doors unguarded.’
sn35.132:12.2 | S: How do you define someone who doesn’t guard the sense doors?” | T: In what way, Master Kaccana, is one ‘with sense doors unguarded’?”
sn35.132:12.3 | S: “Brahmin, take someone who sees a sight with their eyes. If it’s pleasant they hold on to it, but if it’s unpleasant they dislike it. They live with mindfulness of the body unestablished and their heart stunted. | T: “Here, brahmin, having seen a form with the eye, someone is intent upon a pleasing form and repelled by a displeasing form. He dwells without having set up mindfulness of the body, with a limited mind,
sn35.132:12.4 | S: And they don’t truly understand the freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom where those arisen bad, unskillful qualities cease without remainder. | T: and he does not understand as it really is that liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, wherein those evil unwholesome states cease without remainder.
sn35.132:12.5 | S: When they hear a sound with their ears … | T: Having heard a sound with the ear …
sn35.132:12.6 | S: When they smell an odor with their nose … | T: (none)
sn35.132:12.7 | S: When they taste a flavor with their tongue … | T: (none)
sn35.132:12.8 | S: When they feel a touch with their body … | T: (none)
sn35.132:12.9 | S: When they know an idea with their mind, if it’s pleasant they hold on to it, but if it’s unpleasant they dislike it. They live with mindfulness of the body unestablished and their heart stunted. | T: Having cognized a mental phenomenon with the mind, someone is intent upon a pleasing mental phenomenon and repelled by a displeasing mental phenomenon. He dwells without having set up mindfulness of the body …
sn35.132:12.10 | S: And they don’t truly understand the freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom where those arisen bad, unskillful qualities cease without remainder. | T: cease without remainder.
sn35.132:12.11 | S: That’s how someone doesn’t guard the sense doors.” | T: It is in such a way, brahmin, that one is ‘with sense doors unguarded.’”
sn35.132:12.12 | S: “It’s incredible, worthy Kaccāna, it’s amazing! | T: “It is wonderful, Master Kaccana! It is amazing, Master Kaccana!
sn35.132:12.13 | S: How accurately you’ve explained someone whose sense doors are unguarded! | T: How Master Kaccana has declared one whose sense doors are actually unguarded to be one ‘with sense doors unguarded’!
sn35.132:13.1 | S: You also spoke of someone who does guard the sense doors. | T: But Master Kaccana said ‘with sense doors guarded.’
sn35.132:13.2 | S: How do you define someone who does guard the sense doors?” | T: In what way, Master Kaccana, is one ‘with sense doors guarded’?”
sn35.132:13.3 | S: “Brahmin, take a mendicant who sees a sight with their eyes. If it’s pleasant they don’t hold on to it, and if it’s unpleasant they don’t dislike it. They live with mindfulness of the body established and a limitless heart. | T: “Here, brahmin, having seen a form with the eye, someone is not intent upon a pleasing form and not repelled by a displeasing form. He dwells having set up mindfulness of the body, with a measureless mind,
sn35.132:13.4 | S: And they truly understand the freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom where those arisen bad, unskillful qualities cease without remainder. | T: and he understands as it really is that liberation of mind, liberation by wisdom, wherein those evil unwholesome states cease without remainder.
sn35.132:13.5 | S: When they hear a sound with their ears … | T: Having heard a sound with the ear …
sn35.132:13.6 | S: When they smell an odor with their nose … | T: (none)
sn35.132:13.7 | S: When they taste a flavor with their tongue … | T: (none)
sn35.132:13.8 | S: When they feel a touch with their body … | T: (none)
sn35.132:13.9 | S: When they know an idea with their mind, if it’s pleasant they don’t hold on to it, and if it’s unpleasant they don’t dislike it. They live with mindfulness of the body established and a limitless heart. | T: Having cognized a mental phenomenon with the mind, someone is not intent upon a pleasing mental phenomenon and not repelled by a displeasing mental phenomenon. He dwells having set up mindfulness of the body …
sn35.132:13.10 | S: And they truly understand the freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom where those arisen bad, unskillful qualities cease without remainder. | T: cease without remainder.
sn35.132:13.11 | S: That’s how someone guards the sense doors.” | T: It is in such a way, brahmin, that one is ‘with sense doors guarded.’”
sn35.132:14.1 | S: “It’s incredible, worthy Kaccāna, it’s amazing! | T: “It is wonderful, Master Kaccana! It is amazing, Master Kaccana!
sn35.132:14.2 | S: How accurately you’ve explained someone whose sense doors are guarded! | T: How Master Kaccana has declared one whose sense doors are actually guarded to be one ‘with sense doors guarded’!
sn35.132:14.3 | S: Excellent, worthy Kaccāna! Excellent! | T: Magnificent, Master Kaccana! Magnificent, Master Kaccana!
sn35.132:14.4 | S: As if he were righting the overturned, or revealing the hidden, or pointing out the path to the lost, or lighting a lamp in the dark so people with clear eyes can see what’s there, the worthy Kaccāna has made the teaching clear in many ways. | T: The Dhamma has been made clear in many ways by Master Kaccana … as in §127 …
sn35.132:14.5 | S: I go for refuge to the Buddha, to the teaching, and to the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: (none)
sn35.132:14.6 | S: From this day forth, may the worthy Kaccāna remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life. | T: From today let Master Kaccana remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.
sn35.132:14.7 | S: Please come to my family just as you go to the families of the lay followers in Makkarakaṭa. | T: “Let Master Kaccana approach the Lohicca family just as he approaches the families of the lay followers in Makkarakaṭa.
sn35.132:14.8 | S: The brahmin boys and girls there will bow to you, rise in your presence, and give you a seat and water. That will be for their lasting welfare and happiness.” | T: The brahmin youths and maidens there will pay homage to Master Kaccana, they will stand up for him out of respect, they will offer him a seat and water, and that will lead to their welfare and happiness for a long time.”
sn35.132:14.9 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.1
an3.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
an3.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s Park.
an3.1:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus:
an3.1:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Bhikkhus!”
an3.1:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Venerable sir!” those bhikkhus replied.
an3.1:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
an3.1:2.1 | S: “Whatever dangers there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: “Bhikkhus, whatever perils arise all arise on account of the fool, not on account of the wise person.
an3.1:2.2 | S: Whatever perils there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: Whatever calamities arise all arise on account of the fool, not on account of the wise person.
an3.1:2.3 | S: Whatever hazards there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: Whatever misfortunes arise all arise on account of the fool, not on account of the wise person.
an3.1:2.4 | S: It’s like a fire that spreads from a hut made of reeds or grass, and burns down even a bungalow, plastered inside and out, draft-free, with doors fastened and windows shuttered. | T: Just as a fire that starts in a house made of reeds or grass burns down even a house with a peaked roof, plastered inside and out, draft-free, with bolts fastened and shutters closed;
an3.1:2.5 | S: In the same way, whatever dangers there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: so too, whatever perils arise … all arise on account of the fool, not on account of the wise person.
an3.1:2.6 | S: Whatever perils there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: (none)
an3.1:2.7 | S: Whatever hazards there are, all come from the foolish, not from the astute. | T: (none)
an3.1:3.1 | S: So, the fool is dangerous, but the astute person is safe. | T: (1) Thus, bhikkhus, the fool brings peril, the wise person brings no peril;
an3.1:3.2 | S: The fool is perilous, but the astute person is not. | T: (2) the fool brings calamity, the wise person brings no calamity;
an3.1:3.3 | S: The fool is hazardous, but the astute person is not. | T: (3) the fool brings misfortune, the wise person brings no misfortune.
an3.1:3.4 | S: There’s no danger, peril, or hazard that comes from the astute. | T: There is no peril from the wise person; there is no calamity from the wise person; there is no misfortune from the wise person.
an3.1:4.1 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “Therefore, bhikkhus, you should train yourselves thus:
an3.1:4.2 | S: ‘We will shun the three things by which a fool is known, and we will proceed having undertaken the three things by which an astute person is known.’ | T: ‘We will avoid the three qualities possessing which one is known as a fool, and we will undertake and practice the three qualities possessing which one is known as a wise person.’
an3.1:4.3 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: It is in this way that you should train yourselves.”
an3.1:4.4 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.38
an5.38:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a faithful gentleman gets five benefits. | T: “Bhikkhus, these five benefits come to a clansman endowed with faith.
an5.38:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.38:1.3 | S: The true persons in the world show sympathy first to the faithful, not so much to the unfaithful. | T: (1) When the good persons in the world show compassion, they first show compassion to the person with faith, not so to the person without faith.
an5.38:1.4 | S: They first approach the faithful, not so much the unfaithful. | T: (2) When they approach anyone, they first approach the person with faith, not so the person without faith.
an5.38:1.5 | S: They first receive alms from the faithful, not so much the unfaithful. | T: (3) When they receive alms, they first receive alms from the person with faith, not so from the person without faith.
an5.38:1.6 | S: They first teach Dhamma to the faithful, not so much the unfaithful. | T: (4) When they teach the Dhamma, they first teach the Dhamma to the person with faith, not so to the person without faith.
an5.38:1.7 | S: When their body breaks up, after death, the faithful are reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm. | T: (5) With the breakup of the body, after death, a person with faith is reborn in a good destination, in a heavenly world.
an5.38:1.8 | S: A faithful gentleman gets these five benefits. | T: These are the five benefits that come to a clansman who has faith.
an5.38:2.1 | S: Suppose there was a great banyan tree at a level crossroads. It would become a refuge for birds from all around. | T: “Just as at a crossroads on level ground, a great banyan tree becomes the resort for birds all around,
an5.38:2.2 | S: In the same way, a faithful gentleman becomes a refuge for many people—monks, nuns, laywomen, and laymen. | T: so the clansman endowed with faith becomes the resort for many people: for bhikkhus, bhikkhunīs, male lay followers, and female lay followers.”
an5.38:3.1 | S: With its branches, leaves, and fruit, | T: A large tree with a mighty trunk, branches, leaves, and fruit,
an5.38:3.2 | S: a great tree with its strong trunk, | T: (none)
an5.38:3.3 | S: firmly-rooted and fruit-bearing, | T: firm roots, and bearing fruit,
an5.38:3.4 | S: supports many birds. | T: is a support for many birds.
an5.38:4.1 | S: It’s a lovely place, | T: Having flown across the sky, the birds resort to this delightful base:
an5.38:4.2 | S: frequented by the sky-soarers. | T: (none)
an5.38:4.3 | S: Those that need shade go in the shade, | T: those in need of shade partake of its shade;
an5.38:4.4 | S: those that need fruit enjoy the fruit. | T: those needing fruit enjoy its fruit.
an5.38:5.1 | S: So too, a faithful individual | T: Just so,
an5.38:5.2 | S: is perfect in ethics, | T: when a person is virtuous, endowed with faith,
an5.38:5.3 | S: placid in manner and amenable, | T: of humble manner, compliant,
an5.38:5.4 | S: gentle, courteous, and tender. | T: gentle, welcoming, soft,
an5.38:6.1 | S: Those free of greed, freed of hate, | T: those in the world who are fields of merit— devoid of lust and hatred,
an5.38:6.2 | S: free of delusion, undefiled, | T: devoid of delusion, taintless—
an5.38:6.3 | S: fields of merit for the world, | T: (none)
an5.38:6.4 | S: associate with such a person. | T: resort to such a person.
an5.38:7.1 | S: They teach them the Dhamma, | T: They teach him the Dhamma
an5.38:7.2 | S: that casts aside all suffering. | T: that dispels all suffering,
an5.38:7.3 | S: Having understood this teaching, | T: having understood which
an5.38:7.4 | S: the undefiled one is fully extinguished.” | T: the taintless one here attains nibbāna.
an5.38:7.5 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)


